    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Current practices for disease modeling or drug screening for neurodegenerative genetic diseases rely on human immortalized cell lines or animal models that do not accurately represent the progression of the disease or capture the effects drugs will have in clinical trials. Human neurons induced from stem cells have been shown to accurately capture the effects the drugs will have on the diseases neurons and can serve as a reliable model for the progression of these (Jiménez-Moreno et al., 2017; Jungverdorben et al., 2017). However, current methods for obtaining human neurons for drug testing and disease modeling involve converting readily available cell types such as fibroblasts and blood cells into induced pluripotent stem cells and subsequently differentiating into neurons. These methods often involve multiple lengthy and complicated combinations of morphogens or growth factors and produce neurons at a very low efficiency. Our lab previously reported a facile and rapid method to produce induced neuronal (iN) cells by overexpressing a proneural transcription factor, NGN2, in human embryonic stem cells and induced pluripotent stem cells (Zhang et al., 2013).To determine the neuronal subtypes at a single cell level, we first performed single cells RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) for human embryonic stem cells, day 4 and day 28 iN cells. Two transcription factors (ISL1 and PHOX2B) were found to be upregulated in a subpopulation of iN cells which are positive for cholinergic markers such as SLC18A3 and SLC5A7. Immunofluorescence staining of ISL1 and PHOX2B confirmed the scRNA-seq results. We further confirmed overexpression of ISL1 and/or PHOX2B in Ngn2 derived iN cells is sufficient to upregulate in cholinergic genes. The cholinergic fate was partially removed by forcing the induced neurons into a frontal brain neuronal fate that showed an increase in glutamatergic markers. Through this research we have shown that ISL1 and PHOX2B are important regulators of cholinergic cell fate and can be overexpressed or removed to give the desired neuronal cell fate.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
